WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.499 --> 00:00:01.380
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:01.380 --> 00:00:05.060
[PATTING KNEES]

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:08.890
If you don't get physically ill
seeing your first rough cut,

00:00:08.890 --> 00:00:10.627
something's wrong.

00:00:10.627 --> 00:00:12.960
You always think you're not
going to get physically ill,

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:13.460
but you do.

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:16.400
[LAUGHS] Well, if you're
taking this class,

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:19.750
you should have the
bravery and ignorance

00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:21.870
of wanting to get
something done.

00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:23.770
[BELL RING]

00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:26.800
People think it's good
editing if you can see it.

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:30.220
Good editing is something else
too, where you don't see it.

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:33.430
Sometimes, you think something
is dreadfully wrong, and later

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:36.965
and you look at in the
context of a cut, might work.

00:00:36.965 --> 00:00:38.590
Well, in the case of
production design,

00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:41.119
when you take it to
casino, the excess

00:00:41.119 --> 00:00:42.160
is what the film's about.

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:46.150
The manic nature of it,
splashiness of the whole thing.

00:00:46.150 --> 00:00:48.800
We knew that it had a
certain quality to it that

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:51.662
is contrasted later with the
confrontations between Joe

00:00:51.662 --> 00:00:53.620
Pesci and Bob De Niro,
and just the two of them

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:54.790
in the desert arguing.

00:00:54.790 --> 00:00:57.760
You feel the change,
there's nothing there.

00:00:57.760 --> 00:00:59.740
[MECHANICAL NOISES]

00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:01.950
If the actors want to take
a scene certain place,

00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:02.710
I'll go with him there.

00:01:02.710 --> 00:01:04.668
There are things I know
don't work immediately,

00:01:04.668 --> 00:01:07.050
but you shoot it,
because it's a process.

00:01:07.050 --> 00:01:09.000
If you had the
time on set, I like

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:13.274
to tell the actors to try
anything, add the time to fail.

00:01:13.274 --> 00:01:14.690
Just go ahead, try
this, try that.

00:01:14.690 --> 00:01:16.981
You need that freedom, you
need give them that freedom.

00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:21.110
Well of course
when I was younger,

00:01:21.110 --> 00:01:23.270
we didn't have cameras,
and that sort of thing,

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:24.230
it was too expensive.

00:01:24.230 --> 00:01:29.720
This world of movie making,
where these miraculous images

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:33.980
and stories came from
really felt very distant.

00:01:33.980 --> 00:01:35.250
I had a teacher.

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:38.144
He was the one to
give me the confidence

00:01:38.144 --> 00:01:40.310
to become a filmmaker, to
come from this other world

00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:44.340
and suddenly be able
to express myself.

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:47.360
If the machinery of it all
seems too big, and scary,

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:48.930
and overwhelming, it's great.

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:50.930
You wake up in the morning
and you do it anyway.

00:01:53.354 --> 00:01:54.770
You have to learn
it yourself, you

00:01:54.770 --> 00:01:56.603
have to learn it going
through that process.

00:01:56.603 --> 00:01:59.420
That's the greatest thing any
teacher could give a student.

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:02.480
The confidence and the
inspiration to make you think,

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:06.290
well, it's crazy, but
maybe I can do it.

00:02:06.290 --> 00:02:09.199
Now it's time to get to work.

00:02:09.199 --> 00:02:14.050
I'm Martin Scorsese, and
this is my MasterClass.

